WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.740
Sometimes, after you’ve spent two hours
trying to pick a film on Netflix, you begin

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.860
to wonder how the world functions when we
all have so many decisions to make.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:16.099
Elections, referendums, swipe left or right,
sleep in or be on time, raspberry ripple or

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:20.820
mint choc chip, you are constantly forced
to make choices about every aspect of your

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.880
life, from the big to the small.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.660
But it’s hard, isn’t it?

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.089
You have to find a balance between what you
want and what you think is good for you and

00:00:29.089 --> 00:00:30.109
the people around you.

00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:34.390
And there’s so much compromise because you
can’t research every choice so you just

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.770
have to go on the limited information that
you have.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:43.190
So, if you want to know why you broke up with
the love of your life, why you paid to watch

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:49.629
Zoolander 2 and why you got that Brony tattoo,
it’s because you’re bad at making decisions;

00:00:49.629 --> 00:00:53.010
we all are, and this is why…

00:00:53.010 --> 00:01:01.809
History; it’s supposedly something we study
so we can learn from the mistakes of the past.

00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:04.989
The thing is, we don’t really, do we.

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:10.110
We have a very strong tendency to look at
how things have been very recently and ignore

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.350
things that happened a long time ago.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:17.640
If you got food poisoning last week from some
service station sushi, you’d probably be

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.060
pretty cautious about eating any seafood right
now.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.579
But if it was a year ago, you’ll happily
stuff your face with raw fish like a ravenous

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:26.579
seal.

00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:28.659
This is called the recency effect.

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:31.200
Financial investors are a great example of
this.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:37.259
It was greed and wilful blindness that led
the world economy to collapse in 2007, causing

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:39.020
the great recession.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:45.020
So after such a major disaster, we surely
have many more checks and balances in place

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.909
so this can’t happen again.

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:51.079
Yeah… about that… we don’t.

00:01:51.079 --> 00:01:58.039
Nothing really changed, the market recovered
and it’s pretty much business as usual because

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:03.070
the stock market, as a whole, focuses on a
much shorter window.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:09.440
2007 is almost 10 years ago, who cares, stocks
are up now and your broker just bought a toilet

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.760
made of gold; INVEST!

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.000
Your brain is absolutely brilliant at spotting
patterns, that’s a lot of its job, well,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.891
that and remembering lines of annoying songs,
mean things your ex said and places where

00:02:21.891 --> 00:02:24.220
you haven’t left your wallet.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.430
Think about facial recognition; you know your
friends and family’s faces no matter what

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.720
angle you see them from.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:34.130
You’re not matching an exact image from
memory, your brain just sees the pattern of

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:40.470
how their features relate and makes the deduction
that it’s your grandma and not your girlfriend,

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.350
thank god for that.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:46.650
So we often see patterns when there are none,
like spotting shapes in the clouds.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.500
It’s called the Clustering illusion.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.590
We don’t have a good natural appreciation
of the randomness of the universe and we often

00:02:54.590 --> 00:03:00.830
feel that events have to even out on a much
shorter time scale than they actually do.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.950
Take a coin toss for example.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:08.420
On most coins it’s roughly 50/50 whether
you get heads or tails and, over a long enough

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.760
time scale, the results of the flips will
show that.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:18.060
But the previous results have absolutely no
bearing on the outcome of the next flip.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.250
If you flipped something like this
tails, tails, heads, tails, heads, heads,

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:25.150
tails, heads, tails, heads,
…then, everyone’s happy.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.220
But if you flipped ten tails in a row …

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:28.220
tails, tails, tails, tails, tails, tails,
tails, tails, tails, tails.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:32.040
…then there are going to be some eyebrows
raised and some people may think you are actually

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.920
magic or just cheating.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.970
But both these results actually have an equal
probability but if I asked you to bet on the

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:45.990
next flip after, say, five consecutive tails,
you’re far more likely to say heads than

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.930
after just one or two flips of tails.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:53.431
This is called the Gambler’s Fallacy and
is the main reason why you shouldn’t take

00:03:53.431 --> 00:03:55.810
your credit card to the casino.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.240
Another major part of decision making is salience.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:04.230
This just means how noticeable or memorable
something is and the most common area where

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.540
this is a factor is safety.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.660
If you ask many Americans to list the biggest
dangers facing their country, they are very

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.530
likely to list terrorism.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:20.180
Around 3,500 US citizens have been killed
by terrorism in the last 20 years, and although

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:26.080
this is a lot of people, it’s tiny in comparison
to things like car accidents and cancer and

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.320
about the same as the amount of people who
drowned in swimming pools.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:35.060
But major events, such as 9/11, understandably
take up a much more important and memorable

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.640
place in people’s thought processes.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:42.980
No one sits down with a coffee, reads an article
about lung cancer and immediately panics and

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.280
starts shooting smokers.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:51.360
What happens is that extremes, both good and
bad, have a much bigger influence on us than

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:52.360
they should.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:56.830
Say you’d been drinking tequila most weekends
for a few years and the worst that had happened

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.430
was a headache and the occasional awkward
text.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:05.330
But then one Saturday, you go a little overboard
and end up dressed as Pikachu, asking random

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:10.880
girls to capture you in the Pokeball and suddenly
it’s adios tequila for the rest of your

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:11.880
life.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.640
All the good times are forgotten, and that
one massive cringe moment stays forever.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:22.600
This is called salience and it can majorly
skew your decision making.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.540
Since we all find decision making difficult,
for wider issues we often take the easiest

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:32.900
option; just going along with what everyone
else does, this is called the bandwagon effect.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.650
It happens all the time and often causes people
to reject their own opinion for the sake of

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.300
joining the herd.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.800
The fashion industry demonstrates this constantly.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:46.240
Your favourite outfit of ten years ago probably
looks like something you’d wear to a costume

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.120
party now, and it’s not because you suddenly
looked in the mirror and realised that three-quarter

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.810
length shorts and a scarf made you look like
a confused sailor, you just saw that no one

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:59.050
else was wearing it anymore so you took it
off.

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.600
But you really should have burnt it.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.340
Politics is heavily affected by the bandwagon
effect and it’s why the idea of “momentum”

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:08.700
is so important.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:14.880
We, the voters, want to feel like we are on
the winning side, so feeling that a candidate

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.530
is going to win makes people more likely to
vote for them and creates a kind of feedback

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:21.530
loop.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:26.580
You see this in the Presidential primaries
in the US, and the obsession over states like

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:31.080
Iowa and New Hampshire since they vote before
the others.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.710
But I don’t want you to think we are complete
sheep and immediately just follow what the

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.360
majority say.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.600
We also have a donkey like stubbornness when
it comes to changing our minds.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:45.670
The bandwagon effect is strong when someone
is undecided but, once their choice has been

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:51.460
made, a large opposition opinion is actually
more likely to make them double down on their

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:54.830
choice, rather than reverse their position.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:07:00.000
Confirmation bias is our tendency to interpret
everything in a way that fits with what we

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.070
already believe, rather than using new information
to change our position.

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:08.800
If you need an example, just look at every
argument on the internet.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.889
Rather than listening to the other person’s
ideas, we just spend hours on Wikipedia trying

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:17.130
to find something to undermine them and then
hoping that they use the wrong version of

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:21.080
“your/you’re” so we can throw it in
their illiterate face and call their mother

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.220
a Nazi slut, or something like that.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:29.170
There is a book called Dice Man by Luke Rhinehart,
about a psychologist who tests if you can

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:35.290
give up your morals by passing your decision
making over to the roll of a dice, so you

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:40.450
can say “hey, it wasn’t my choice to steal
this bagel, the dice told me to do it”.

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:44.650
And if you get away with stealing the bagel
then you might begin to think you have hit

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:47.750
on some brilliant decision making process.

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:50.270
This is called Outcome bias.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:54.620
If you had a multiple choice test and you
decided to choose by always picking the longest

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:59.030
answer, and then you got an A on that test,
you would be inclined to think that this was

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:01.340
a good way to do all tests.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:06.610
This positive outcome would justify your stupid
tactic and you might decide to do it again

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:09.060
because, hey, it worked last time.

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:13.740
It’s similar to the idea of lucky objects;
just because that underwear got you through

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.919
one exam, please don’t wear them all the
way through finals, you’ll get some sort

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:19.660
of infection.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:24.210
Outcome bias is often confused with hindsight,
and although they have a lot in common, hindsight

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:29.980
is about memory, in that you change the story
of what happened after you see the result,

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:34.670
making it seem like you knew what was going
to happen all along; no you’re not the messiah,

00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:37.159
you’re a very naughty boy.

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:42.500
And finally we come to anchoring, which is
probably something truly awful on urbandictionary.com.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:48.029
Basically, you are a little limpet and you
like to cling to the first rock that you come

00:08:48.029 --> 00:08:49.029
across.

00:08:49.029 --> 00:08:53.480
The first information that you get on a subject
is often likely to carry much more weight

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:54.480
than it should.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.500
It’s a useful tool in negotiation since
the first amount someone says is then the

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:03.540
starting point and then both sides dance around
that number.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:08.240
So if you want to haggle for a low price,
make sure you are the first one to throw out

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:09.240
a number.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:13.580
Anchoring also has a big impact in tests and
surveys since if you begin the question with

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:17.550
something negative, you are more likely to
get a negative answer.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:23.300
Unsurprisingly, politicians use this to huge
effect all the time, using a seemingly innocent

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:27.360
poll to actually influence the way people
are voting.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:34.180
So now you are left with a conundrum; which
is more important recency or anchoring?

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:40.750
We talked most recently about anchoring but
we anchored on recency… are you doubting

00:09:40.750 --> 00:10:03.010
your decision making yet?

